   304→          <h2>About the 2D Graphics Engine</h2>
   305→
   306→          <h3>What is this?</h3>
   307→          <p>
   308→            This is a <strong>2D graphics API</strong> similar to OpenGL, but focused on 2D rendering instead of 3D. Just like OpenGL provides functions for drawing triangles and applying textures in 3D space, this engine provides a complete API for 2D drawing operations.
   309→          </p>
   310→          <p>
   311→            It's a software renderer built from scratch in C++, implementing functionality found in production libraries like <strong>Skia</strong> (used by Chrome and Android), <strong>Cairo</strong> (used by GTK and Firefox), and the <strong>HTML5 Canvas API</strong>. You give it high-level drawing commands—"draw a rectangle," "fill a path with a gradient," "apply a matrix transformation"—and it produces the actual pixel colors on screen.
   312→          </p>
   313→          <p>
   314→            <strong>Key Features:</strong>
   315→          </p>
   316→          <ul style={{ lineHeight: 1.8, color: '#555' }}>
   317→            <li><strong>Shape Drawing:</strong> Rectangles, polygons, paths with curves</li>
   318→            <li><strong>Transformations:</strong> 2D affine matrices (translate, rotate, scale) with CTM stack</li>
   319→            <li><strong>Shaders:</strong> Solid colors, linear/radial/sweep gradients, bitmap textures with tile modes</li>
   320→            <li><strong>Compositing:</strong> Porter-Duff blend modes (12 modes including src-over, xor, multiply)</li>
   321→            <li><strong>Path Rendering:</strong> Winding fill rule, Bezier curve tessellation</li>
   322→            <li><strong>Advanced Geometry:</strong> Triangle meshes, Coons patches, shader composition</li>
   323→          </ul>
   324→
   325→          <h3>Technologies Used</h3>
   326→          <ul style={{ lineHeight: 1.8, color: '#555' }}>
   327→            <li><strong>C++17:</strong> Core rendering engine implementation with hand-optimized rasterization algorithms</li>
   328→            <li><strong>WebAssembly:</strong> Compiles C++ to bytecode that runs at near-native speed in the browser</li>
   329→            <li><strong>Emscripten:</strong> Toolchain for compiling C++ to WebAssembly and generating JavaScript bindings</li>
   330→            <li><strong>HTML5 Canvas API:</strong> Display target for the rendered pixel buffer (ARGB → RGBA at 60fps)</li>
   331→          </ul>
   332→
   333→          <h3>The Rendering Pipeline</h3>
   334→          <p>
   335→            Every draw call flows through this pipeline, transforming high-level commands into pixel colors:
   336→          </p>
